First time setup
----------------

To get the BSP you need to have `repo` installed and its prerequisites.
This only needs to be done once.

Using an Ubuntu 20.04 machine, install the following dependencies:
- python 2.x - 2.6 or newer ($: sudo <pkg-mgr> install python)
- git 1.8.3 or newer ($: sudo <pkg-mgr> install git)
- curl ($: sudo <pkg-mgr> install curl)
where <pkg-mgr> is the package manager for your distribution
(apt-get for Debian/Ubuntu, yum or dnf for CentOS/Fedora, zypper for Suse).

Install the `repo` utility:

$: mkdir ~/bin

$: curl http://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/git-repo-downloads/repo  > ~/bin/repo

$: chmod a+x ~/bin/repo

$: PATH=${PATH}:~/bin


Download the Yocto Project Environment into your machine:
-----------------------------------------------------------
$: mkdir grayvip_yocto

$: cd grayvip_yocto

$: repo init -u ssh://git@bitbucket.sw.nxp.com/grayvip/grayvip-manifests.git -b develop -m default-bitbucket.xml

$: repo sync

Preparing your environment
-----------------------------------------------------------
To install all prerequisites before starting any build, run:

$: ./sources/meta-alb/scripts/host-prepare.sh

Setting up the VIP environment:
-----------------------------------------------------------
Now you can create a build directory in the root by running the command:

$: source nxp-setup-alb.sh -D fsl-grayvip -m s32n79sim -e "meta-grayvip meta-vip meta-aws meta-java"

Building the VIP image:
-----------------------------------------------------------
You can now build the VIP image using:

$: bitbake fsl-image-grayvip

Once the build is finalized, the build artifacts will be present in the `build_s32n79sim/tmp/deploy/images`.